WHAT TO DO:
- Keep the subject exactly as asked. You are expanding a request, not replacing it.
- Supply the specifics the template needs, in the brief. Real figures, real names, real thresholds. If the request says "what a 70B model costs at home", the brief should already contain the parameter count, the bytes per parameter, what a common card holds, and roughly what the hardware costs — because the writer needs those numbers and will otherwise make them up.
- Take specifics from the sources above FIRST, where they are given. Reach for your own knowledge only where they are silent.
- Use widely-known, checkable facts. Where a number varies, say the range or the assumption ("at 16-bit, so two bytes a parameter").
- Keep the viewer's angle. If the request implies a question — should I, can I, how much — the brief should keep asking it.
- Two to five sentences of prose. This is an instruction, not the script.
- Do not write narration, headings, or beats. Do not mention the template by name.
- Do not enumerate. No numbered points, no bulleted list, no "first... second... third". Whatever shape you write, the writer downstream copies — a brief in five parts becomes five beats of eight words each, which blows the length budget and reads as a list being read aloud. Fewer, fuller sentences.

DO NOT INVENT ANY OF THESE, EVER:
- A named product, company or person that does not exist. If you need an example, name a real one.
- A named case study, customer, or success story — "a developer named X built Y and got thousands of downloads" is a fabrication even when it sounds typical, and it is the fastest way to destroy the credibility of a whole video.
- A statistic, threshold or ceiling you do not actually know. A template that needs a number it cannot get is the wrong template: say so plainly in the brief instead, and let the writer work with what is real. An invented figure is worse than an absent one, because the finished clip renders it as fact.

If the request genuinely does not suit this template, still write the best brief you can for it: say plainly what the closest honest version of this subject is in this format. Do not refuse and do not return the request unchanged.

Respond with a single JSON object and nothing else:
{"prompt": "the fuller brief, two to five sentences", "notes": "one line on what you added"}
